\b Friday + OpenMetadata + Snowflake: Detailed End-to-End Document\b0\par
\par
\b 1) Objective\b0\par
This document explains the complete runtime flow for Friday (Teams bot), including OpenMetadata integration, Redis caching behavior, LLM orchestration, SQL execution in Snowflake, tool usage, and operational best practices.\par
\par
\b 2) Current Environment (Configured)\b0\par
- Teams bot endpoint through ngrok tunnel to local gateway\par
- Gateway server: FastAPI + Bot Framework adapter\par
- LLM: openai-compatible endpoint (gpt-4o-mini)\par
- OpenMetadata URL: http://65.21.48.174:8585\par
- OpenMetadata database schema FQN: Teams_Gateway.ONSEMI.LABIQ\par
- Snowflake DB/Schema: ONSEMI / LABIQ\par
- Redis: redis://localhost:6379/0\par
\par
\b 3) High-Level Architecture\b0\par
\par
{\f1
User (Teams)\line
  -> Bot Framework Service\line
  -> Teams Gateway (FastAPI + Bot Handler)\line
  -> Agent Runtime\line
      -> OpenMetadata Client (schema + tags + metadata)\line
      -> Redis Cache (schema/context/history/state/tokens)\line
      -> LLM Planning + Tool Calls\line
      -> Snowflake Executor (read-only SQL)\line
  -> Streaming response back to Teams\line
}\par
\par
\b 4) End-to-End Runtime Flow (Sample)\b0\par
\b User query:\b0  "What are the top 5 products by total revenue?"\par
\par
\b Step 1: Teams ingress\b0\par
1. User sends message in Teams.\par
2. Bot Framework validates incoming token and forwards activity payload to gateway endpoint.\par
\par
\b Step 2: Handler pre-processing\b0\par
1. Gateway extracts user id + conversation id.\par
2. AAD id is mapped to email using AAD_TO_EMAIL mapping.\par
3. Handler starts streamed reply placeholder and invokes agent runtime.\par
\par
\b Step 3: Agent bootstrapping\b0\par
1. Agent checks if conversation is new.\par
2. Agent requests schema context from OpenMetadata client.\par
\par
\b Step 4: Schema resolution with cache\b0\par
1. OpenMetadata client checks Redis key: tmcp:om:schema_context.\par
2. If cache hit: returns schema immediately (no OpenMetadata API call).\par
3. If cache miss: calls OpenMetadata APIs, builds schema context, stores in Redis with TTL.\par
\par
\b Step 5: LLM planning\b0\par
1. Agent sends system prompt + schema context + user message to LLM.\par
2. LLM chooses tool call (execute_sql) with read-only SQL.\par
\par
\b Step 6: SQL safety + execution\b0\par
1. SQL validator enforces read-only policy (SELECT/WITH/SHOW/DESCRIBE/EXPLAIN only).\par
2. Snowflake executor runs validated SQL on ONSEMI.LABIQ.\par
3. Result is normalized and returned to agent.\par
\par
\b Step 7: Final response generation\b0\par
1. Tool result is sent back to LLM.\par
2. LLM formats concise Teams-friendly output (table/summary).\par
3. Gateway streams final answer to Teams user.\par
\par
\b 5) Redis: What is Cached and Why\b0\par
\par
\b Key\tab Purpose\tab Typical TTL\b0\par
- tmcp:om:schema_context\tab formatted schema context for prompt\tab OPENMETADATA_SCHEMA_CACHE_TTL (currently 300s)\par
- tmcp:om:tables\tab raw table metadata payload\tab OPENMETADATA_SCHEMA_CACHE_TTL\par
- tmcp:om:jwt_token\tab OpenMetadata login token\tab ~3000s (50 min)\par
- tmcp:conv:*\tab conversation history\tab HISTORY_TTL_SECS\par
- tmcp:state:*\tab per-conversation state (last rows, etc.)\tab conversation lifecycle\par
\par
\b Important behavior\b0\par
Redis is always checked first, even for first-time user messages.\par
- First ever request after flush: cache miss -> fetch from OpenMetadata -> cache.\par
- Subsequent requests before TTL expiry: cache hit -> no OpenMetadata call.\par
- After TTL expiry: cache miss again -> refresh from OpenMetadata.\par
\par
\b 6) Why OpenMetadata (vs direct Snowflake metadata only)\b0\par
Direct Snowflake metadata browsing typically gives tables, columns, and data types.\par
OpenMetadata adds governance and semantic intelligence:\par
- Column/table descriptions (business meaning)\par
- PII/sensitivity tags and classifications\par
- Lineage information\par
- Ownership + stewardship context\par
- Profiling + quality metadata\par
- Unified catalog across multiple systems\par
\par
\b Top 10 high-value OpenMetadata capabilities\b0\par
1. Auto-discovery ingestion\par
2. PII/sensitivity classification\par
3. Lineage visibility\par
4. Data quality integration\par
5. Profiling statistics\par
6. Business glossary mapping\par
7. Multi-source catalog\par
8. Governance policies\par
9. Alerts/monitoring\par
10. Usage insights\par
\par
\b 7) Tools in Friday Runtime\b0\par
- execute_sql: Executes validated read-only SQL in Snowflake.\par
- local__to_csv: Exports table-like output to CSV attachment.\par
- local__chart: Produces chart image from tabular result sets.\par
- teams__get_chat_summary: Reads Teams messages for summarization/analysis.\par
\par
\b 8) Security and Guardrails\b0\par
1. Token validation at Bot Framework ingress.\par
2. Read-only SQL guardrails in validator.\par
3. Rate limits + request body limits.\par
4. Audit logging and telemetry around tool calls/errors.\par
5. Conversation and metadata caching with TTL controls.\par
\par
\b 9) Operational Notes\b0\par
- If schema changes in OpenMetadata are not reflected immediately, wait for TTL expiry or clear Redis.\par
- If chart/file preview breaks in Teams, verify attachment handling path and Teams-compatible URL/content type behavior.\par
- Keep OpenMetadata token and schema cache TTLs tuned for performance vs freshness.\par
\par
\b 10) Troubleshooting Quick Guide\b0\par
\b Symptom:\b0  "No tables available"\par
\b Check:\b0  OPENMETADATA_DATABASE_FQN and OpenMetadata permissions/service names.\par
\par
\b Symptom:\b0  SQL compilation errors using service-prefixed names\par
\b Check:\b0  Ensure LLM uses Snowflake table names directly and not OpenMetadata service namespace in SQL.\par
\par
\b Symptom:\b0  Slow first response\par
\b Check:\b0  This is expected on cache miss (OpenMetadata fetch + token refresh).\par
\par
\b Symptom:\b0  Metadata appears stale\par
\b Check:\b0  OPENMETADATA_SCHEMA_CACHE_TTL and Redis key refresh behavior.\par
\par
\b 11) Recommended Defaults\b0\par
- OPENMETADATA_SCHEMA_CACHE_TTL=300 (balanced freshness/performance)\par
- HISTORY_MAX_TURNS=30 (good short-term memory window)\par
- SNOWFLAKE_QUERY_TIMEOUT_SECS=60 (reasonable ceiling)\par
- RATE_LIMIT_* tuned by expected user concurrency\par
\par
\b 12) Final Summary\b0\par
Friday uses OpenMetadata as the semantic control plane and Snowflake as the execution plane.\par
Redis provides low-latency caching for schema, tokens, and conversation state.\par
The result is a governed, read-only, context-aware assistant that answers data questions safely in Teams.\par
\par
\b End of document\b0\par
